1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,400
How can a bridge move while hundreds
of ants are walking over it?

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:09,200
Some army ants build living roadways
by linking their bodies across gaps

3
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:12,800
in the forest floor. Their nestmates
stream over the structure

4
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:17,200
instead of following a longer detour.
But the bridge keeps changing.

5
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:22,800
In field experiments with Eciton hamatum,
researchers watched bridges lengthen,

6
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:26,000
widen and shift toward the main trail.
The movement increased

7
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,800
when traffic was heavier. The likely
mechanism is local: workers in busier

8
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:35,600
sections are more likely to be joined
by passing ants, while less-contacted

9
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:40,400
workers are more likely to leave.
No ant needs to know the whole route.

10
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:44,800
The shortcut saves travel, but every ant
holding the bridge is temporarily

11
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:50,400
unavailable for foraging. So the structure
settles where the colony's traffic benefit

12
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:54,400
is balanced against the workers it ties up.
